// §14 entry 17 -- `exsc aedifica --hospes riscv64-linux SOURCE` from
// x86_64, byte-identical to a native CI build -> byte-identical output,
// cross-host. SHAPE is byte-identical-output (like entry 16), not
// reject-with-a-code.
//
// §9.5: "`exsc` with no `--hospes` is an error. No default-to-build-
// platform... Native compilation is the case where build == host, spelled
// out." §9.2: "a C target that cannot honour the declared `numeri` fails
// the build rather than degrading silently" -- entry 17 is the same
// reference-vs-target agreement requirement applied to a full
// cross-compiled build's OUTPUT rather than one numeric policy.
//
// The SOURCE under test does not need anything target-specific for this to
// be meaningful -- the property is that the SAME source, compiled
// `--hospes riscv64-linux` on an x86_64 build machine, produces the same
// bytes a native riscv64 CI runner would produce for it -- so this is
// deliberately the same ordinary, already-valid file as entry 16.
//
// DEFERRED, current blocker: no riscv64 reference backend exists, and
// there is no native riscv64 (or equivalent CI) build to compare against.

publica functio saluta() -> textus {
    redde "Ave, mundus.";
}

